(note "**Genitive and dative.** A Greek case ending rendered with an English preposition (\"of the humans\" for τῶν ἀνθρώπων) belongs to that noun's segment and is not bracketed: the word is in the Greek, as an ending. Only words with nothing behind them in the Greek are bracketed.")

(note "**Tense and aspect in the literal layer.** Present: English progressive (\"is shining\"). Imperfect: \"was ...ing\", except εἰμί, which has no aorist (\"was\"). Aorist: simple past (\"came to be\"; negated, \"did not overtake\"). Perfect: \"has ...\". The auxiliaries that carry tense are part of the verb's segment, not supplied words.")
